,1124,701,323,2661,429,17767,282,62,22,284,220,23,18,1124,701,582,1184,311,8253,279,2750,315,17767,282,62,16,1124,8,323,17767,282,62,17,1124,8,1156,4192,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,2610,1231,312,18883,55856,279,3491,323,18130,279,6291,504,279,7167,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $f_1$, $f_2$, $f_3$, $\\dots$ be a sequence of numbers such that\n\\[\n  f_n = f_{n - 1} + f_{n - 2}\n\\]for every integer $n \\ge 3$.  If $f_7 = 83$, what is the sum of the first 10 terms of the sequence?\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve for the sum of the first 10 terms of the sequence \\( f_1, f_2, f_3, \\ldots \\) defined by the recurrence relation \\( f_n = f_{n-1} + f_{n-2} \\) for \\( n \\geq 3 \\), and given that \\( f_7 = 83 \\), we need to determine the values of \\( f_1 \\) and \\( f_2 \\) first.\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"5785e5fb7c4199059b56616e81d5376580758004364498388f38ecc383b58dfe"},"seconds":66.80516576766968,"seed":1063679418583642801,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the sum of the first 10 terms of the sequence \\( f_1, f_2, f_3, \\ldots \\) defined by the recurrence relation \\( f_n = f_{n-1} + f_{n-2} \\) for \\( n \\geq 3 \\), and given that \\( f_7 = 83 \\), we need to determine the values of \\( f_1 \\) and \\( f_2 \\) and then calculate the sum.\n\nFirst, let's express the terms of the sequence in terms of \\( f_1 \\) and \\( f_2 \\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_3 &= f_2 + f_1, \\\\\nf_4 &= f_3 + f_2 = f_2 + f_1 + f_2 = f_1 + 2f_2, \\\\\nf_5 &= f_4 + f_3 = f_1 + 2f_2 + f_2 + f_1 = 2f_1 + 3f_2, \\\\\nf_6 &= f_5 + f_4 = 2f_1 + 3f_2 + f_1 + 2f_2 = 3f_1 + 5f_2, \\\\\nf_7 &= f_6 + f_5 = 3f_1 + 5f_2 + 2f_1 + 3f_2 = 5f_1 + 8f_2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nGiven that \\( f_7 = 83 \\), we have:\n\\[\n5f_1 + 8f_2 = 83.\n\\]\nWe need to find integer solutions for \\( f_1 \\) and \\( f_2 \\). Let's try some values:\nIf \\( f_2 = 7 \\), then:\n\\[\n5f_1 + 8 \\cdot 7 = 83 \\implies 5f_1 + 56 = 83 \\implies 5f_1 = 27 \\implies f_1 = \\frac{27}{5},\n\\]\nwhich is not an integer. So, \\( f_2 = 7 \\) is not a solution.\n\nIf \\( f_2 = 8 \\), then:\n\\[\n5f_1 + 8 \\cdot 8 = 83 \\implies 5f_1 + 64 = 83 \\implies 5f_1 = 19 \\implies f_1 = \\frac{19}{5},\n\\]\nwhich is not an integer. So, \\( f_2 = 8 \\) is not a solution.\n\nIf \\( f_2 = 6 \\), then:\n\\[\n5f_1 + 8 \\cdot 6 = 83 \\implies 5f_1 + 48 = 83 \\implies 5f_1 = 35 \\implies f_1 = 7,\n\\]\nwhich is an integer. So, \\( f_1 = 7 \\) and \\( f_2 = 6 \\) is a solution.\n\nNow we can calculate the terms up to \\( f_{10} \\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_3 &= f_2 + f_1 = 6 + 7 = 13, \\\\\nf_4 &= f_3 + f_2 = 13 + 6 = 19, \\\\\nf_5 &= f_4 + f_3 = 19 + 13 = 32, \\\\\nf_6 &= f_5 + f_4 = 32 + 19 = 51, \\\\\nf_7 &= f_6 + f_5 = 51 + 32 = 83, \\\\\nf_8 &= f_7 + f_6 = 83 + 51 = 134, \\\\\nf_9 &= f_8 + f_7 = 134 + 83 = 217, \\\\\nf_{10} &= f_9 + f_8 = 217 + 134 = 351.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe sum of the first 10 terms is:\n\\[\nf_1 + f_2 + f_3 + f_4 + f_5 + f_6 + f_7 + f_8 + f_9 + f_{10} = 7 + 6 + 13 + 19 + 32 + 51 + 83 + 134 + 217 + 351 = 913.\n\\]\nSo, the sum of the first 10 terms of the sequence is \\(\\boxed{913}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{913}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To find the sum of the first 10 terms of the sequence \\( f_1, f_2, f_3, \\ldots \\) defined by the recurrence relation \\( f_n = f_{n-1} + f_{n-2} \\) for \\( n \\geq 3 \\), and given that \\( f_7 = 83 \\), we need to determine the values of \\( f_1 \\) and \\( f_2 \\) and then calculate the sum.\n\nFirst, let's express the terms of the sequence in terms of \\( f_1 \\) and \\( f_2 \\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_3 &= f_2 + f_1, \\\\\nf_4 &= f_3 + f_2 = f_2 + f_1 + f_2 = f_1 + 2f_2, \\\\\nf_5 &= f_4 + f_3 = f_1 + 2f_2 + f_2 + f_1 = 2f_1 + 3f_2, \\\\\nf_6 &= f_5 + f_4 = 2f_1 + 3f_2 + f_1 + 2f_2 = 3f_1 + 5f_2, \\\\\nf_7 &= f_6 + f_5 = 3f_1 + 5f_2 + 2f_1 + 3f_2 = 5f_1 + 8f_2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nGiven that \\( f_7 = 83 \\), we have:\n\\[\n5f_1 + 8f_2 = 83.\n\\]\nWe need to find integer solutions for \\( f_1 \\) and \\( f_2 \\). Let's try some values:\nIf \\( f_2 = 7 \\), then:\n\\[\n5f_1 + 8 \\cdot 7 = 83 \\implies 5f_1 + 56 = 83 \\implies 5f_1 = 27 \\implies f_1 = \\frac{27}{5},\n\\]\nwhich is not an integer. So, \\( f_2 = 7 \\) is not a solution.\n\nIf \\( f_2 = 8 \\), then:\n\\[\n5f_1 + 8 \\cdot 8 = 83 \\implies 5f_1 + 64 = 83 \\implies 5f_1 = 19 \\implies f_1 = \\frac{19}{5},\n\\]\nwhich is not an integer. So, \\( f_2 = 8 \\) is not a solution.\n\nIf \\( f_2 = 6 \\), then:\n\\[\n5f_1 + 8 \\cdot 6 = 83 \\implies 5f_1 + 48 = 83 \\implies 5f_1 = 35 \\implies f_1 = 7,\n\\]\nwhich is an integer. So, \\( f_1 = 7 \\) and \\( f_2 = 6 \\) is a solution.\n\nNow we can calculate the terms up to \\( f_{10} \\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_3 &= f_2 + f_1 = 6 + 7 = 13, \\\\\nf_4 &= f_3 + f_2 = 13 + 6 = 19, \\\\\nf_5 &= f_4 + f_3 = 19 + 13 = 32, \\\\\nf_6 &= f_5 + f_4 = 32 + 19 = 51, \\\\\nf_7 &= f_6 + f_5 = 51 + 32 = 83, \\\\\nf_8 &= f_7 + f_6 = 83 + 51 = 134, \\\\\nf_9 &= f_8 + f_7 = 134 + 83 = 217, \\\\\nf_{10} &= f_9 + f_8 = 217 + 134 = 351.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe sum of the first 10 terms is:\n\\[\nf_1 + f_2 + f_3 + f_4 + f_5 + f_6 + f_7 + f_8 + f_9 + f_{10} = 7 + 6 + 13 + 19 + 32 + 51 + 83 + 134 + 217 + 351 = 913.\n\\]\nSo, the sum of the first 10 terms of the sequence is \\(\\boxed{913}\\).","id":"intermediate_algebra:573"}
